From 2fd18e2a1ddc8c25ed333b58692e1ef44eff3a88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 23:45:55 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] common : --split-balance for speed-aware layer splits

A layer split runs the devices one after another for every token, so
splitting by free memory (the default) is the slowest choice when the
devices differ in speed: on gabesrv10 (8060S) + gabesrv06 (780M, RPC)
it put 33% of Qwen3.8-27B on the slow GPU, giving pp8192 204 t/s and
tg 7.2 t/s vs 297/12.1 on one node.

New modes:
  memory   split by free memory (previous behavior)
  decode   fill the fastest devices first
  prefill  balance pipeline stage times
  auto     minimize predicted time of a --split-workload P:G request
           (default 4096:256); the default

Each device is timed with synthetic matmuls shaped like the model's
layers (decode: single-token, prefill: n_ubatch-token), remote RPC
devices over the normal protocol with a readback, plus RPC hop latency
and bandwidth. Results are cached (--split-calibrate cache|force).
Memory caps come from the fit projection; the chosen split is verified
against it and shrunk on overflow. An explicit -ts always wins.

tests: test-split-balance checks the optimizer against brute force and
the expected decode/prefill/auto behavior.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
